+        "code": "import numpy as np\n\ndef transform(grid):\n    \"\"\"\n    The rule is to tile the input grid horizontally and vertically.\n    The number of tiles is determined by the output dimensions.\n    Looking at the demos:\n    Demo 1: 3x3 -> 9x9 (3x3 tiles)\n    Demo 2: 3x3 -> 6x6 (2x2 tiles)\n    Demo 3: 3x3 -> 12x12 (4x4 tiles)\n    Demo 4: 3x3 -> 6x6 (2x2 tiles)\n    Demo 5: 3x3 -> 9x9 (3x3 tiles)\n    \n    The number of tiles is (Output_Dim / Input_Dim).\n    However, since we don't know the output dimension, we look for a pattern.\n    Wait, the output dimension is always a multiple of the input dimension.\n    In all demos, the output is a tiling of the input grid.\n    The number of repetitions is (Output_Dim / Input_Dim).\n    \n    Actually, the rule is simpler: the output is a tiling of the input grid.\n    The number of repetitions is determined by the fact that the output is \n    always a square (or rectangle) where the dimensions are multiples of \n    the input dimensions.\n    \n    Let's re-examine:\n    Demo 1: 3x3 -> 9x9 (3 repetitions)\n    Demo 2: 3x3 -> 6x6 (2 repetitions)\n    Demo 3: 3x3 -> 12x12 (4 repetitions)\n    Demo 4: 3x3 -> 6x6 (2 repetitions)\n    Demo 5: 3x3 -> 9x9 (3 repetitions)\n    \n    Wait, there is no fixed number of repetitions. Let's look at the input \n    values. Is there a clue?\n    Demo 1: Input has 9, 7, 6. Output is 9x9.\n    Demo 2: Input has 3, 4. Output is 6x6.\n    Demo 3: Input has 8, 2, 1, 3. Output is 12x12.\n    Demo 4: Input has 7, 2. Output is 6x6.\n    Demo 5: Input has 2, 3, 1. Output is 9x9.\n    \n    Actually, the number of repetitions is the sum of the unique colors \n    present in the input grid?\n    Demo 1: {9, 7, 6} -> 3 colors. 3x3 tiles.\n    Demo 2: {3, 4} -> 2 colors. 2x2 tiles.\n    Demo 3: {8, 2, 1, 3} -> 4 colors. 4x4 tiles.\n    Demo 4: {7, 2} -> 2 colors. 2x2 tiles.\n    Demo 5: {2, 3, 1} -> 3 colors. 3x3 tiles.\n    \n    This fits perfectly!\n    \"\"\"\n    unique_colors = len(np.unique(grid))\n    \n    h, w = grid.shape\n    # Create a tiled array\n    # np.tile repeats the array along the specified axes\n    tiled_grid = np.tile(grid, (unique_colors, unique_colors))\n    \n    return tiled_grid",
