eated.\n    How to find it?\n    D1: 9x9 / 3x3 = 3x3 tiles.\n    D2: 6x6 / 3x3 = 2x2 tiles.\n    D3: 12x12 / 3x3 = 4x4 tiles.\n    D4: 6x6 / 3x3 = 2x2 tiles.\n    D5: 9x9 / 3x3 = 3x3 tiles.\n    \n    Notice a pattern? \n    D1: Input has 3 unique values? No.\n    D1: Input has 3 rows?\n    D2: Input has 3 rows?\n    \n    Let's look at the grid values.\n    D1: 9, 7, 6. (3 colors)\n    D2: 3, 4. (2 colors)\n    D3: 8, 2, 1, 3. (4 colors)\n    D4: 7, 2. (2 colors)\n    D5: 2, 3, 1. (3 colors)\n    \n    The multiplier k = number of unique colors in the input grid!\n    D1: Colors {9, 7, 6} -> 3 colors. Multiplier 3. Output 3*3 x 3*3.\n    D2: Colors {3, 4} -> 2 colors. Multiplier 2. Output 2*3 x 2*3.\n    D3: Colors {8, 2, 1, 3} -> 4 colors. Multiplier 4. Output 4*3 x 4*3.\n    D4: Colors {7, 2} -> 2 colors. Multiplier 2. Output 2*3 x 2*3.\n    D5: Colors {2, 3, 1} -> 3 colors. Multiplier 3. Output 3*3 x 3*3.\n    \n    This fits perfectly!\n    \"\"\"\n    unique_colors = len(np.unique(grid))\n    k = unique_colors\n    \n    h, w = grid.shape\n    # Create a tiled array\n    # np.tile repeats the array k times along axis 0 and k times along axis 1\n    return np.tile(grid, (k, k))",
